WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.719
Speaker 1: Marjorie Taylor GREENE radical
Republican congresswoman, said yesterday that

00:00:03.719 --> 00:00:06.690
Tucker Carlson's filing firing by Fox News
was illegal.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:11.660
Now, she doesn't use the word illegal, but
it's really important to understand when these

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.990
people on the right say that was a violation
of the First Amendment.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.300
They are saying that it is against the law,
what took place.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.180
We're going to look at a video clip of Marjorie
Taylor GREENE explaining, I guess, her perspective,

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.029
if you can call it that.

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:28.330
What is really important to understand is
that when she says Tucker's First Amendment

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:33.270
rights were violated by Fox News, she's saying
it was an illegal firing.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:40.610
These are the people who hold as one of their
most important stated values, low business

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:41.610
regulation.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.180
Let businesses do what they want to do.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.180
Law and order.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.800
We need to unburden businesses from government
limitations.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.400
They should be allowed to do what is best
and most profitable for them.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.710
And here, she says, a firing by a private
company was unconstitutional and therefore

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:57.710
against the law.

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:00.050
We're going to break it all down.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:01.380
Let's take a listen.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.710
Speaker 2: Big news of the week.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:05.049
Fox fired Tucker Carlson.

00:01:05.049 --> 00:01:06.380
Fox has gone left.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:07.380
They've gone.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.380
Woke.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.710
We're clearly calling them out for it.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:11.420
Tucker is a great voice.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.080
Speaker 4: For conservatives across the country.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:17.110
Speaker 2: He doesn't have the platform anymore.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:21.950
When you weigh in on Fox's decision to fire,
probably one of the more conservative voices,

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:23.440
most important voices in America.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:24.440
Absolutely.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.410
I think it was devastating to our First Amendment.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:29.080
Eric, I see it as a very serious problem.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.650
Our First Amendment is free speech, but it's
also freedom of press.

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:37.000
And when a gigantic media company like Fox
News fires their number one.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.520
Tucker Carlson, not for him doing anything
wrong, not because his ratings were bad, but

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.850
they literally fired him because they came
to the mob.

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:47.700
Speaker 1: Now, that's completely untrue.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.909
Tucker Carlson caused a multi-hundred million,
if not multibillion dollar problem for Fox

00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:52.909
News.

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:55.329
Fox News made this decision based on money.

00:01:55.329 --> 00:01:59.240
Not only was Tucker's program partially responsible
for the news coverage that got them sued by

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:06.450
Dominion and Smartmatic and so far led to
a nearly $800 million lawsuit settlement,

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:07.840
rather, in favor of dominion.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.090
He also is being sued by a former staffer
for alleged discrimination and sexism and

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:12.180
so on and so forth behind the scenes.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.290
He potentially will have caused another lawsuit
by Ray Epps that may be forthcoming.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:19.569
This was a financial thing, but that's not
the focus of this segment.

00:02:19.569 --> 00:02:21.880
It's Marjorie's misunderstanding of the First
Amendment.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:30.000
Speaker 2: What we're seeing is we're seeing
the end of the First Amendment.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.200
That's how we got it.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.970
I can't see it any other way.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:34.260
We've seen censorship out of control.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:43.510
We we saw the federal government, Department
of Homeland Security align with big tech to

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:48.760
censor Americans for several years now on
political issues because they weren't speaking

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:49.760
the speech.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:50.980
That was the chosen speech to say.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:51.980
Well, neither was Tucker Carlson.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:55.160
Tucker Carlson was pushing the limit every
single night on his show, and he was telling

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.010
the news stories that the American people
wanted to hear.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:02.470
I also want to point out that many Democrats
even watch Tucker Carlson.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:03.470
Okay.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:08.629
Speaker 1: So the question here is, is there
any way in which a company company firing

00:03:08.629 --> 00:03:13.769
an employee could violate the First Amendment
the way Marjorie Taylor GREENE says the First

00:03:13.769 --> 00:03:16.290
Amendment of the Constitution to the Constitution
protects individuals?

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:20.530
And in theory, companies as well, from government
actions that interfere with freedom of speech,

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:21.989
religion, press, assembly and petition.

00:03:21.989 --> 00:03:22.989
Private companies.

00:03:22.989 --> 00:03:26.349
Private companies are not bound by the First
Amendment in the same way.

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:31.030
And on the other hand, they also generally
have the right to set their own rules and

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:32.030
policies.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:34.830
This is something that people like Marjorie
Taylor GREENE on the right claim to revere

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:40.069
as one of the highest goals and virtues of
the United States legal system.

00:03:40.069 --> 00:03:42.480
And this applies to employment as well.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:43.819
Now, could firings be illegal?

00:03:43.819 --> 00:03:44.819
Sure.

00:03:44.819 --> 00:03:50.670
There's all sorts of wrongful termination
that that can and often is argued by by employees.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:56.989
If Tucker were to be fired because he's white
or because he's Christian, certainly he would

00:03:56.989 --> 00:04:00.159
have a very good case for wrongful termination.

00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:04.189
I don't believe it would have anything to
do with the First Amendment.

00:04:04.189 --> 00:04:06.370
It would violate employer nondiscrimination
law.

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:11.100
But the idea that because they fired Tucker,
the First Amendment has been violated merely

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.340
because Tucker spoke on TV is ridiculous.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:14.340
It's ridiculous.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:15.580
And it shouldn't even be.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:20.560
The sad thing is that we have to explain it
in this way.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:30.590
Now, is there any way is there any way where
a company could be violating the First Amendment

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:32.560
rights of an employee?

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.270
Imagine that an employee speaks out about
a matter of public concern as a citizen, and

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:41.470
their speech isn't related to their job duties
in any way.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:46.060
You can try to argue that your First Amendment
rights were violated.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:51.530
It's very tough to do if it's a government
job.

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:56.770
It's slightly easier to do, but it's a tough
it's a tough standard to reach.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:05:01.620
Some states have laws that protect employees
above and beyond the First Amendment.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:06.389
Like, for example, some states have laws that
prohibit employers from retaliating against

00:05:06.389 --> 00:05:08.590
employees for political activity outside of
work.

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:13.620
So that would be a situation where there is
an additional protection and the employee

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.670
would be able to hold their employer to account.

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:21.440
Do any of these circumstances even remotely
apply to Fox News firing Tucker Carlson?

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:22.530
Not Absolutely not.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:23.530
Absolutely not.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:26.800
And so we are faced once again with this question.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:33.370
Do right wingers not actually know what the
First Amendment does and what it is, or do

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:38.180
they know but they realize their followers
don't, and so they blatantly lie to them on

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:39.180
TV.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:40.180
I don't know.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:43.940
You be the judge, but what we do know is Marjorie
Taylor GREENE is humiliatingly wrong here.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:48.639
Today, I'm going to be speaking with Max Burns,
who's an award winning Democratic strategist

00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:50.830
and also founder of Third Degree Strategies.

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:56.669
Max recently wrote an article asserting that
we may we may finally be at a tipping point

00:05:56.669 --> 00:06:01.690
when it comes to gun violence and gun safety
in the United States.

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:03.310
We're going to talk about that.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:06.030
Max, great to have you on.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.449
I appreciate it.

00:06:07.449 --> 00:06:11.250
Speaker 4: Thanks so much for having me.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:18.850
Speaker 1: So, I mean, listen, my audience
knows we've heard this so many times before.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:20.800
This this is definitely a tipping point.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:25.360
You know, Sandy Hook was a tipping point or
Pulse nightclub or whatever else the case

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:26.360
may be.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.280
Even popular reforms like a background check
as a requirement for any gun transaction,

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:31.490
including private person to person sales.

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.640
Very popular with the American public after
Sandy Hook can't get it done.

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:38.120
So I think people would be right to be skeptical
that we are now at a tipping point so late

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:39.440
laid out for us.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:40.440
Why now?

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:41.440
What has changed?

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:43.069
Speaker 4: Well, you're absolutely right.

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:47.690
I think the gun violence conversation has
been one that's been littered with disappointments

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:48.690
from the beginning.

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:53.380
But what we're seeing now is a really interesting
confluence of factors.

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:58.850
One is that even though Congress is essentially
useless on gun policy right now, we're seeing

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:03.060
a real golden age of Democratic lawmakers
in states passing legislation that would have

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:04.550
been unimaginable even a few years ago.

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:10.210
Gretchen Whitmer, for example, just passed
an 11 bill gun reform package that would have

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:12.370
ended her career back in 2008.

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:16.280
Now they're talking about her as a potential
candidate for president.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:19.660
The other side of this is the NRA.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:20.771
They are in freefall.

00:07:20.771 --> 00:07:23.830
And what was once the biggest gun rights group
in America can now barely afford to keep the

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:31.020
lights on in its office, much less fund the
candidates that have distorted this conversation

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:32.020
for decades.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:37.090
So there's this this unique window of weakness
on the right and strength on the left that's

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:40.360
really creating a unique space for a gun conversation
that leads to actual legislation.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:44.539
Speaker 1: I think a couple of the difficulties
are and part of this relates to what is or

00:07:44.539 --> 00:07:52.190
could be done at the federal level and what
is or could be done at the state level.

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:58.680
A lot of this right now, for example, when
you talk about the Gretchen Whitmer package,

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:01.379
we're talking about the state level.

00:08:01.379 --> 00:08:05.330
And here there is a sort of like moral hazard
situation where the state's most willing to

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:08.509
deal with this stuff often are the ones that
already are kind of ahead on the stricter

00:08:08.509 --> 00:08:11.470
side when it comes to gun safety as it is.

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:15.960
So then when we think about, well, how do
we deal with this issue in South Carolina

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:17.389
or Texas, we start to think federally.

00:08:17.389 --> 00:08:19.240
And one of the difficulties about federal
gun safety legislation is that running on

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:23.620
it for national candidates often isn't a great
idea from a strategy perspective, which I

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:26.729
would be glad to let you speak to as well.

00:08:26.729 --> 00:08:32.479
But we there's this idea and maybe it's true,
maybe it's not, that when you run national

00:08:32.479 --> 00:08:34.240
campaigns, including for like Senate.

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:35.240
Right.

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:39.630
Because you're in D.C. but your constituents
are in your state, that when you talk about

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:45.460
gun safety regulations, you rarely bring new
voters to support you, but you often activate

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:47.410
people who disagree with you.

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:54.010
And so there's that difficulty of saying,
well, the way to get Texas and West Virginia

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:57.210
and South Carolina to do something is to go
federal.

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:59.790
But campaigning on this stuff federally often
backfires.

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:01.720
So how do you do that?

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:04.120
Speaker 4: Yeah, I think that's a great point.

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:07.339
And one of the interesting challenges here
is that for years Democrats were sort of bought

00:09:07.339 --> 00:09:11.830
into this Republican message that it was political
suicide to talk about guns in a campaign.

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:12.830
Right.

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:13.980
I remember following the Virginia Tech shooting.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:15.170
Democrats came out the day after to publicly
reassure America that they wouldn't dream

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:17.450
of passing any gun control legislation.

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:21.040
And now those same people are leading major
campaigns in states and federally on gun reform.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:27.029
And that's in large part due to the rise of
groups like Everytown Moms Demand Action,

00:09:27.029 --> 00:09:32.279
These new groups that have sort of adopted
the right's organizing approach for this.

00:09:32.279 --> 00:09:37.750
And we see that now that the majority of Americans
have in some way been touched by gun violence.

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:40.540
Numbers in support of gun reform are at record
highs.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:45.170
And we're seeing that really start to break
down the old coalition.

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:50.160
In Tennessee, the Republican governor, Lee,
is supporting red flag legislation.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:53.700
In Utah, the Republican governor, alongside
Everytown, passed a bunch of gun reforms that

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:57.140
make it tougher to get a gun and require background
checks.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:00.620
And that's a coalition that was unimaginable
even a few years ago.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:06.680
The Republicans are seeing the pressure mount
and actually realizing it's safer to take

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:08.660
incremental gun safety steps.

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:11.010
This is a completely different landscape than
I think Republicans are used to playing in.

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:16.110
And it's one where Democrats can actually
talk about guns and find that people are agreeing

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:18.790
with them for the first time since 1994.

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:25.020
Speaker 1: What is the timeline on which you
think we could see action on some of these

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:26.020
areas?

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:30.780
Speaker 4: I think it's much quicker than
than we think if we're looking at states.

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:35.430
The more that we've organized and this is
a really key part of every town, is they are

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:38.730
funding state races, they're cheaper, they
have a bigger impact on actual legislation.

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:43.709
And we're seeing that start to create a momentum
for national change once red states, which

00:10:43.709 --> 00:10:48.470
are disproportionately affected by gun violence,
have seen that these things work, they're

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:51.040
actually pretty excited to talk about them.

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:54.290
And that's something that we're seeing distorted
by the gun lobby.

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:59.310
But when voters go to the polls, they are
voting on this issue.

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:01.339
It's a big reason why.

00:11:01.339 --> 00:11:09.550
You see Joe Biden has gone from saying he
won't talk about social issues at all in 2024

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:10.550
to opening with an ad that talks heavily about
gun violence.

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:11.740
Democrats can see it's a winning issue and
they're for once really finding their voice.

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:12.740
Here.

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:13.740
Speaker 1: We have in the United States sort
of three separate gun issues.

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:14.740
You have mass shootings.

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:15.740
You have individual homicides affecting one
or two individuals per incident, and then

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:16.740
you have suicides using firearms in thinking
about.

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:18.110
And the prescriptions would be different for
these three different categories.

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:22.480
If we think about mass shootings for a moment,
I've put out a list of ten or 12 different

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:23.480
ideas that would never eliminate every mass
shooting, but would certainly do something

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:24.480
to reduce the number.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:27.640
And there are some trends that we see when
it comes to the mass shootings.

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:29.477
Age of the shooter, typically under 25 type
of firearm used, often an assault style rifle.

00:11:29.477 --> 00:11:30.477
You know, there's these trends that we start
to see.

00:11:30.477 --> 00:11:31.477
What's the lowest hanging fruit in your mind
that would blend the most effective changes

00:11:31.477 --> 00:11:33.330
that could be made with ones that are most
likely to actually be politically viable?

00:11:33.330 --> 00:11:35.230
Speaker 4: Well, I think there's this unique
moment here that we see with Tennessee, with

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:36.230
Utah, with North Dakota that's talking about
raising the age for gun ownership, that you

00:11:36.230 --> 00:11:37.230
combine background checks, which we've seen
even in in Michigan was a hugely popular thing.

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:38.230
Universal background check used to be a consensus
Republican position in the Bush administration.

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:39.230
They have gone so far off on that that it
doesn't resemble anything Americans want.

00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:40.680
But you combine that background check legislation
and red flag legislation and you have immediately

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:42.899
handled two of the most vulnerable populations,
people who are going to use their guns for

00:11:42.899 --> 00:11:43.899
violence against others knowingly at the time
they buy the gun.

00:11:43.899 --> 00:11:48.390
And people who are going to use it for self
harm disproportionately young people.

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:49.390
And in rural communities, in agricultural
communities, huge rates have gone suicide.

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:50.550
And it all has to do with gun access.

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:54.030
And we can talk about making sure guns aren't
in the hands of kids without talking about

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:55.030
these Republican conspiracies of stealing
everyone's guns away.

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:56.030
And we're finding that the space for that
conversation is getting bigger every.

00:11:56.030 --> 00:11:57.030
Speaker 1: Year when it comes to the next.

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:58.030
What's remaining, I guess I would say, of
the Biden first term.

00:11:58.030 --> 00:11:59.030
And, you know, we'll see whether there's a
second term and sort of what framing he uses

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:01.649
for the 2024 campaign, given the circumstances
in the House and Senate, Republicans controlling

00:12:01.649 --> 00:12:05.550
the House, Democrats controlling the Senate
at this point.

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:07.955
What's your sense of at the federal level,
what might be possible?

00:12:07.955 --> 00:12:08.955
Know, I understand you're saying that Senate
Republicans I'm sorry, that state Republicans

00:12:08.955 --> 00:12:09.955
to some degree seem more willing to take action.

00:12:09.955 --> 00:12:11.680
What about things that would have to go through
the House and Senate?

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:14.981
Speaker 4: So that's the really big sticking
point here, is that Republicans have already

00:12:14.981 --> 00:12:18.769
said that the bipartisan gun bill that they
gave Joe Biden is it for them.

00:12:18.769 --> 00:12:20.190
They do not want any more.

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:23.560
So it's going to be basically a status quo
situation until voters in 2024 can be heard.

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:29.610
Yeah, And one of the reassuring things we're
seeing is that just in the same way that Beltway

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:30.640
consultants said don't talk about abortion
in 2022, it'll be polarizing.

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:33.750
But that turned out to be one of the leading
issues for voters.

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:36.080
We're seeing the same thing now with guns.

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:39.430
And it's one reason why Joe Biden, who is,
as I'm sure you would agree, very risk averse,

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:40.850
is comfortable opening his reelection campaign,
talking about gun violence.

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:42.440
Speaker 1: Do you have any insights and this
is getting very specific now.

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:46.300
You know, there's something we can look at
the gun safety issue, mental health, video

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:47.300
games, all the stuff that is always thrown
around.

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:48.300
But there is something intangible maybe about
American gun culture that doesn't exist in

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:49.300
some of the other countries with relatively
high rates of gun ownership.

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:50.300
Wherein I don't know.

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:51.300
It's just sort of like it's more common in
the U.S. that someone would see firearms as

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:52.300
a way to solve a personal problem and interpersonal
problem, a societal problem that doesn't seem

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:53.300
to be the case in many other countries.

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:54.300
Is there anything that can be done to restructure
that relationship between Americans and guns,

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:55.300
which seems to be sort of like a layer that's
above all the policy that could be put in

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:56.300
place?

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:57.300
Speaker 4: There is.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:58.300
I mean, it's fundamentally a constitutional
issue.

00:12:58.300 --> 00:12:59.300
I'm old enough to remember when people were
blaming video games for violence.

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:00.300
But the problem has always been that guns
in this country often have more rights than

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:01.300
the people they're killing.

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:02.300
And the fact is, the position has moved to
such a maximalist one on the right.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:03.300
That doesn't reflect anything.

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:04.300
Even a few years ago that the court held on
guns.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:05.300
There is now essentially Republicans arguing
for an unlimited right to own any gun that

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:06.300
you want.

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:07.300
And they're saying that anyone, even Republicans
who propose commonsense limits, are against

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:08.300
democracy and against the Constitution.

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:09.300
And it's polarizing people to violence, because
when you're told your fundamental rights are

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:10.300
being taken by a tyrannical government that
justifies almost anything in order to protect

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:11.300
your rights.

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:12.300
And we're seeing that happen.

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:13.300
I mean, we're seeing form of MAGA voters in
Nevada who shot at elected officials they

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:14.300
thought were stealing an election until the
Supreme Court or Congress gets some constitutional

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:15.300
momentum to rethink the madness of our current
interpretation of the Second Amendment.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:16.300
It's tough to get anything long term accomplished.

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:17.300
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to ask you about.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:18.300
I have heard from many progressives, including
friends of mine, but also people who just

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:19.300
call in to my show who express views very
similar to mine on the issue of gun safety

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:20.300
and what they would like to see, but also
don't like the idea of the right wingers being

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:21.300
the only ones with the guns.

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:22.300
And so they have armed themselves, particularly
in the last few years.

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:23.300
They have become progressive gun owners, gladly
willing to subject themselves to background

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:24.300
check, mental health, civil liability insurance,
all the things we would want to put in.

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:25.300
But they don't like the idea of the right
wingers being the ones who disproportionately

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:26.300
have the guns.

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:27.300
Have you seen this?

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:28.300
Have you heard it?

00:13:28.300 --> 00:13:29.300
Is it a race to the bottom or a logical reaction
to what we're seeing out in the world?

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:30.300
Speaker 4: Oh, I've absolutely heard it.

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:31.300
In fact, I believe the fastest growing group
of gun owners is black women.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:32.300
And they're doing this in response to the
increasing racial polarization and race baiting

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:33.300
on the right.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:34.300
And I'll tell you one thing that is sure to
get us into the courts is the idea of black

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:35.300
people owning guns is not something Republicans
consider to be the Second Amendment.

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:36.300
We saw that with Philando Castile, who was
an NRA member that they did not stand up for

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:37.300
at all.

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:38.300
The quickest way that we can get to a meaningful
gun reform conversation is for Republicans

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:39.300
to get scared at all of the non-Republicans
who are buying guns.

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:40.300
But it to me, a completely rational response,
and it's a sign of government policy failure

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:41.300
that people feel the need to have multiple
guns to protect themselves from their own

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:42.300
government's inability to act, to protect
them.

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:43.300
And we should be ashamed of that.

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:44.300
Speaker 1: We are going to link to Max's piece
about this issue.

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:45.300
We've been speaking with Max Burns, who's
a Democratic strategist and also founder of

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:46.300
Third Degree Strategies.

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:47.300
Max, really appreciate your time and insights
today.

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:47.306
Speaker 4: Thank you so much.

